  about: [
    `<em>Second Treatise of Government</em> is the founding document of liberal political thought. Published anonymously in 1689, in the aftermath of the Glorious Revolution, it argues — against divine-right kings, against Hobbes, against every tradition of absolutism — that legitimate government rests only on the consent of the governed, exists only to protect life, liberty, and property, and may be dissolved by the people when it betrays its trust. All men are by nature free, equal, and independent. No one can be put under the authority of another without his own consent. Government holds power in trust, and the trust can be broken.`,
    `What makes the argument dangerous, and what made it the handbook of two revolutions, is that it is rigorous. Locke does not assert rights; he derives them — from the state of nature, from self-ownership, from the labour theory of property, from the logic of consent — until by chapter 19 he has constructed a watertight case for the right to dissolve a government that has become tyrannical. The American colonists recognized the logic. Jefferson paraphrased it, almost word for word, in 1776.`,
  ],

  chaptersSubtitle: 'All 19 chapters, from the state of nature to the right of revolution.',
  chaptersLead: `<p>The Second Treatise builds a sustained argument from first principles. Chapters 1–4 clear the ground: Filmer is wrong, the state of nature is not war, slavery is not legitimate. Chapters 5–9 construct the positive theory: property, family, society, and the ends of government. Chapters 10–14 describe the structure of legitimate government: forms, legislative limits, executive, federative, and prerogative powers. Chapters 15–18 distinguish legitimate authority from its counterfeits — paternal, despotical, conquest, usurpation, and tyranny. Chapter 19 is the payoff: when government dissolves itself and the right of revolution falls back to the people.</p>`,

  themesByline: 'Five threads through the argument',
  themesLead: `The Second Treatise is not a drama but a demonstration. Every chapter moves the argument forward. These five threads run through all nineteen, surfacing again and again as Locke approaches the same questions from different angles.`,

